  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Scattered severe thunderstorms are forecast to \ndevelop late this afternoon and evening across portions of New \nJersey and eastern Pennsylvania. Damaging wind gusts, large \nhail, and a tornado or two will be possible.\n\nUpper ridging will strengthen to our southwest today. A frontal\nboundary will lift just north of the area by this afternoon as a\nshortwave trough approaches from the Great Lakes region by this\nevening. This is expected to result in scattered thunderstorms\ndeveloping in the vicinity of the frontal boundary by late\nafternoon and especially into the evening hours.\n\nThe relatively slow moving shortwave trough and frontal \nboundary sinking back into the area from the northeast into the \nlate evening and overnight hours may support isolated to \nscattered showers and thunderstorms persisting well into the \novernight hours before diminishing toward dawn. However, the \ngreatest severe thunderstorm risk will be with the evening \nactivity, which will occur with the greatest diurnally driven \ninstability coinciding with the greatest upper support and \nshear for convective development as the shortwave trough \narrives.\n\nTiming for severe thunderstorms appears to be mainly between\nabout 5 PM and midnight tonight, though cannot totally rule out\nan isolated storm popping up before that time frame. As\npreviously mentioned, convection will likely linger into the\novernight period, though the severe risk will have largely ended\nby then.\n\nStorms will initially be focused near where ever the frontal\nboundary stalls out this afternoon, which guidance generally\nhas placed across northern New Jersey and into northeastern\nPennsylvania. As storms develop, cold pools will eventually\nstart forcing a modified frontal boundary toward the southwest\nin the direction of the Lehigh Valley, Philly metro, and coastal\nNew Jersey. As this occurs, new storms will likely develop along\nthe boundary into the aforementioned areas farther southwest.\n\nInstability will be quite high by early September standards,\nupwards of 2000-3000+ J/kg MLCAPE. This combined with deep layer\nshear on the order of 35-45 kts will support well organized \nconvective development, with discrete supercells and multicell\nclusters favored. A few localized bowing line segments could\nalso materialize. Mid level lapse rates are not overly\nimpressive, mainly around 6.5 C/km. Low level shear is also not\noverly impressive (~10-15 kts), especially owing to relatively \nweak boundary layer winds, though the shear profile is such that\nlow level hodograph curvature and SRH (100-200 m2/s2) will be \nsupportive of a tornado risk. The tornado risk will be maximized\nwith any discrete cells that can latch onto the frontal boundary\nor any antecedent outflow boundaries.\n\nDamaging winds will be the primary threat with this setup,\nthough large hail and a couple tornadoes are also notable \nthreats. Storms should be moving fast enough to preclude any\nsignificant threat of flash flooding, however any brief training\nof cells could quickly drop 1-3\" of rainfall. This may pose a\nlocalized flash flood threat for urbanized areas, and any areas\nthat have received excessive rainfall in recent days.\n\nSPC has maintained a Slight Risk (level 2 out of 5) for much of\nour NJ and PA areas, as well as northern DE. The probability of\nany storms developing at all is lower farther south into \nDelmarva, though cannot totally rule out an isolated storm \nthere.\n\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Hot and humid conditions are forecast across \nthe region today and again on Thursday. A Heat Advisory is in \neffect for the Philadelphia urban corridor for today. The \ntemperature forecast for Wednesday continues to trend cooler, \nhowever it will remain humid.\n\nThe broad upper level ridging building to our southwest this\nweek will bring hotter and humid conditions reaching into\nportions of our area starting today. Afternoon highs are\nforecast to range from the mid 80s north of I-78 to the upper\n80s and low to even some mid 90s for most areas south of I-78, \nabout 5-10 degrees above average for early September. Dewpoints \nwill also remain mainly in the low 70s. This will lead to heat\nindices climbing into the upper 90s to low 100s for many areas\nsouth of I-78. Forecast maximum heat indices are right near 100\ndegrees for the Philadelphia urban corridor and south into far\nsouthern NJ and Delmarva. Since this is right at Heat Advisory\ncriteria for the Philadelphia urban corridor, we've opted to go\nahead with a targeted advisory for this area. The Heat Advisory\nis in effect from noon through 8 PM today.\n\nA frontal boundary is anticipated to sink into the area by\nWednesday, where it will likely remain much of the day. This\nwill keep temperatures in check, at least for our NJ and PA\ncounties. Forecast high temperatures are mainly in the mid 70s\nto mid 80s, though portions of Delmarva could make a run at the\nlow 90s. Dewpoints will still remain in the upper 60s to low\n70s, so this won't exactly feel like a huge improvement",
